WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:03.040
Some compounds smell really good.

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.930
Because of the chemicals they contain, cinnamon
and vanilla have a pleasant odor that you

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:08.910
might call aromatic.

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:13.720
When an organic chemist uses this word, they’re
likely referring to “aromaticity,” which

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:16.309
is a special feature of some compounds.

00:00:16.309 --> 00:00:21.409
Now, I don’t want to imply that all molecules
with aromaticity smell good; pyridine is an

00:00:21.409 --> 00:00:27.369
aromatic compound with an unpleasant, fish-like
odor, and this aromatic pyridine salt has

00:00:27.369 --> 00:00:29.119
no scent at all.

00:00:29.119 --> 00:00:33.630
Additionally, compounds that are not aromatic
can have strong odors.

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:38.270
So if aromaticity isn’t tied to smell, how
can we tell if a compound is aromatic?

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:44.210
Let’s begin by talking about benzene, the
classical example of an aromatic compound.

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:48.180
We can draw a resonance form for benzene where
it looks like the double bonds have moved.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:50.640
But this isn’t the whole story at all.

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:55.060
There is a continuous system of p-orbitals
above and below the ring.

00:00:55.060 --> 00:01:01.390
The electrons are actually delocalized around
the ring, circling around the pi system.

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:06.329
An electron density map of benzene shows red
“hot spots” of electron density where

00:01:06.329 --> 00:01:10.860
the pi electrons are concentrated.

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:15.679
Although some benzene representations seem
to have alternating single and double bonds,

00:01:15.679 --> 00:01:22.350
each bond is 1.39 Å long, which is between
the length of a carbon-carbon double and single

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:23.350
bond.

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:28.450
This is due to the delocalization of electrons
we discussed, and it makes aromatic compounds

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:30.659
rather stable.

00:01:30.659 --> 00:01:38.190
To be aromatic, a cyclic compound must have
a continuous pi system, and be planar.

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:44.279
It must also satisfy Huckel’s rule, having
4n+2 electrons in the pi system, where n is

00:01:44.279 --> 00:01:46.679
zero or a small, positive number.

00:01:46.679 --> 00:01:53.569
Now, ompounds that contain 4n electrons in
the pi system are particularly unstable, and

00:01:53.569 --> 00:01:56.729
are referred to as antiaromatic.

00:01:56.729 --> 00:02:01.939
We need to be able to identify aromatic compounds
because they have unique reactivity.

00:02:01.939 --> 00:02:06.679
When an alkene is treated with bromine, the
double bond acts as a nucleophile, forming

00:02:06.679 --> 00:02:09.500
a cation that is attacked by Br minus.

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:13.670
The end result is addition of Br2 across the
double bond.

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:18.660
When benzene is treated with bromine, an iron
catalyst is required because of benzene’s

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:19.660
stability.

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:23.580
Benzene attacks bromine; but, Br minus does
not add to the cation.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:28.910
The stability gained by the compound becoming
aromatic again is so great that the intermediate

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:34.140
is deprotonated instead, resulting in the
addition of just one bromine atom.

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:37.880
Let’s explore some more examples of aromatic
compounds.

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:43.260
When n=0, there are two electrons in the pi
system, and an example is the cyclopropenyl

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:44.260
cation.

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:51.030
If n=1, there are six electrons in the pi
system, and benzene and pyridine are examples.

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:55.870
Pyridine is a weak base, because it has a
lone pair that does not participate in the

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:57.610
pi system.

00:02:57.610 --> 00:03:02.980
It sticks out perpendicular, or orthogonal,
to the pi electrons.

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:06.740
Orthogonal lone pairs can make bonds without
disrupting aromaticity.

00:03:06.740 --> 00:03:12.910
So, this methyl-substituted pyridinium ion,
like the pyridine salt we saw earlier, is

00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:14.380
also aromatic.

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:17.370
Pyrimidine is another example.

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:21.731
It has two orthogonal lone pairs and three
double bonds in the ring making up the pi

00:03:21.731 --> 00:03:22.740
system.

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:28.260
Pyrimidine rings are very important because
they make up your DNA.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:32.680
The cyclopentadienyl anion is an aromatic
5-membered ring.

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:38.300
The negative charge on carbon is a lone pair
that can participate in the pi system.

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:42.240
Building on this, pyrrole is also an aromatic
compound.

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:45.400
At first glance, it appears that there are
only 4 pi electrons.

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:51.070
However, there is a lone pair on nitrogen
that can participate in the pi system.

00:03:51.070 --> 00:03:56.570
Furan is also aromatic because one of the
oxygen lone pairs contributes.

00:03:56.570 --> 00:04:03.340
Geometrically, the second lone pair can’t
join, so we have 6 pi electrons again here.

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:07.290
This positively charged seven-membered ring
is also aromatic.

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:12.200
The carbocation is trigonal planar; the empty
p orbital occupies the space above and below

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:17.600
the ring, making a continuous pi system.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:22.260
When we get into 10 electron pi systems, things
get a little more complicated.

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:26.220
Cyclodecapentaene fits most of our criteria
for aromaticity.

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:30.680
It satisfies Huckel’s rule, and is cyclic
with a continuous pi system.

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:36.910
However, in order to be flat, it would need
to have bond angles of 144 degrees, which

00:04:36.910 --> 00:04:43.690
is far from the normal bond angle for an sp2
hybridized carbon–carbon bond, so the strain

00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:48.380
overcomes any aromatic stabilization, and
this molecule bends.

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:53.820
Ok, so what if we introduce some trans double
bonds to relieve the angle strain?

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:56.520
We run into a different problem here.

00:04:56.520 --> 00:05:01.120
Because the H-atoms are now facing inward
toward each other, they have steric clash

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:03.750
and this molecule is not planar either.

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:09.530
But, if the H atoms are replaced with a methylene
bridge, the steric hindrance is eliminated,

00:05:09.530 --> 00:05:12.460
and this molecule is aromatic.

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:17.340
Returning to our chart, this eight-membered
ring with two nitrogen atoms is another example

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:21.210
of a 10-pi electron aromatic compound.

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:25.370
Because both nitrogen lone pairs are needed
to complete the pi system, they have to be

00:05:25.370 --> 00:05:30.740
substituted with electron donating groups;
if the groups are too electron withdrawing,

00:05:30.740 --> 00:05:33.480
the lone pairs are pulled out of the pi system.

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:40.790
The molecule twists because aromatic stabilization
can no longer overcome steric strain.

00:05:40.790 --> 00:05:45.870
This 9-membered ring is also aromatic, again
provided that the nitrogen is substituted

00:05:45.870 --> 00:05:50.039
with electron donating groups and can contribute
to the pi system.

